### The rules every collection follows

- Allocator as a template parameter, `A = ca::heap` by default: `dynamic_array<int, arena_allocator>`
  lives in an arena; `ca::make_in` builds one over any allocator or resource.
- Construction allocates nothing and cannot fail. Everything that acquires memory (`append`,
  `reserve`, `insert`, `clone`, ...) returns a `ca::result` the caller must look at.
- `cleanup()` releases the memory and cleans up the elements that are themselves cleanable,
  returning the first failure. The destructor is only a failsafe.
- No copy constructor where copying allocates: `clone()` returns a `result`.
- `operator[]` traps out of range, in every build; `get(i)` returns an optional for an index that
  may be out of range. Removing functions (`pop`, `remove`) return optionals.
- The API follows Python where it fits: `append`, `extend`, `pop`, `insert`, `remove`, `count`,
  `find`; strings have the str methods, maps the dict ones.
- Element sequences share the slice methods of `borrowed_slice` (layer 2): `prefix`, `sub`, `find`,
  `contains`, `count`, `reverse`, `fill`, `==`; strings share the text methods of `sv`.

```c++
#include "ca/dynamic_array.hpp"
#include "ca/format.hpp"
#include "ca/hashmap.hpp"
#include "ca/str.hpp"

auto main(void) -> int {
	ca::dynamic_array<int> scores{};
	if (!scores.extend({70, 85, 92}) || !scores.append(64))
		return 1;
	ca::hashmap<ca::str, int> best{};
	if (!best.set("ada", scores[2]) || !best.set("bob", scores.first().value_or(0)))
		return 2;
	auto line = ca::format("{} players, best {}", best.size(), best["ada"]);
	if (!line || *line != ca::sv::str{"2 players, best 92"})
		return 3;
	return scores.cleanup() && best.cleanup() && line->cleanup() ? 0 : 4;
}
```

Each container header opens with a performance analysis: layout, cost per operation, growth,
locality, relocation, and measurements against the std equivalent, regenerated by
`make bench-doc`.
